1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,253
We trained a small language model from scratch for Track

2
00:00:03,253 --> 00:00:05,574
01 of the Global Innovation Build Challenge.

3
00:00:05,574 --> 00:00:12,576
The rules cap it at fifty million trainable parameters, and ours has 49,822,228.

4
00:00:12,576 --> 00:00:17,270
It learned from random weights on 20 billion tokens of openly licensed English

5
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:21,061
text, with our own tokenizer, on one H100 in about eight hours.

6
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:27,221
The model is a 14-layer transformer, 512 wide, with grouped-query attention,

7
00:00:27,221 --> 00:00:32,048
rotary position embeddings and QK-norm, and a squared ReLU in the MLP.

8
00:00:32,048 --> 00:00:35,655
U-net style skips connect early layers to late ones, and a value

9
00:00:35,655 --> 00:00:38,867
residual lets every layer reuse the first layer's values.

10
00:00:38,867 --> 00:00:42,782
The 32k embedding is tied to the output head and takes a third of the budget.

11
00:00:42,782 --> 00:00:50,399
Counted once, as the one tensor the optimizer updates, the total is 49,822,228.

12
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:53,523
Counted twice, it is 66.6 million.

13
00:00:53,523 --> 00:00:54,478
We report both.

14
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:58,749
Training follows the modded-nanogpt speedrun recipe.

15
00:00:58,749 --> 00:01:01,907
Muon updates the matrices inside the blocks, and AdamW

16
00:01:01,907 --> 00:01:04,129
updates the embedding and the scalars.

17
00:01:04,129 --> 00:01:08,822
The learning rate warms up, holds, then decays to zero over the last 35 percent of

18
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:12,942
training, and at that point the data switches to higher quality sources.

19
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:17,369
We also average the last few snapshots, and report whichever weights score better.

20
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:22,459
The data is FineWeb-Edu, DCLM, FineMath, FinePDFs and

21
00:01:22,459 --> 00:01:25,755
Wikipedia, all public and openly licensed.

22
00:01:25,755 --> 00:01:30,518
WikiText-103 is built from Wikipedia, which is copied all over the web.

23
00:01:30,518 --> 00:01:33,790
Before filtering, about five percent of its test 13-grams

24
00:01:33,790 --> 00:01:36,201
appeared in a sample of our training data.

25
00:01:36,201 --> 00:01:41,983
So we drop every document sharing any 13-gram with WikiText validation or

26
00:01:41,983 --> 00:01:47,211
test, 4,969 documents in all, plus 16 Wikipedia articles by title.

27
00:01:47,211 --> 00:01:49,969
The multiple-choice benchmarks are not filtered, but

28
00:01:49,969 --> 00:01:52,356
their measured overlap is a handful of items.

29
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:56,837
Before spending eight GPU-hours on a main run, we ran eleven

30
00:01:56,837 --> 00:02:00,115
one-billion-token experiments, each changing one thing.

31
00:02:00,115 --> 00:02:02,431
Muon beat AdamW by a wide margin.

32
00:02:02,431 --> 00:02:07,483
The value residual gave the biggest perplexity gain, 57.2 down

33
00:02:07,483 --> 00:02:11,395
to 54.0, for 13 extra parameters, so we kept it.

34
00:02:11,395 --> 00:02:14,461
The quality anneal raised ARC-Easy but lowered PIQA,

35
00:02:14,461 --> 00:02:16,761
so our two main runs try two strengths.

36
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:20,287
Each arm is one seed, so benchmark gaps under about two points

37
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:23,075
are noise, and we leaned on perplexity to decide.

38
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:27,291
Here are the final numbers for the submitted model, [FINAL

39
00:02:27,291 --> 00:02:30,316
NUMBERS: run], trained on the full 20 billion tokens.

40
00:02:30,316 --> 00:02:32,961
HellaSwag, [FINAL NUMBERS] percent.

41
00:02:32,961 --> 00:02:35,489
ARC-Easy, [FINAL NUMBERS] percent.

42
00:02:35,489 --> 00:02:38,018
PIQA, [FINAL NUMBERS] percent.

43
00:02:38,018 --> 00:02:40,924
WinoGrande, [FINAL NUMBERS] percent.

44
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:44,433
WikiText-103 perplexity, [FINAL NUMBERS].

45
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:49,605
[FINAL NUMBERS: one sentence on how this compares with the 1B run.] All benchmarks

46
00:02:49,605 --> 00:02:54,083
are zero-shot, on the full evaluation sets, with lm-evaluation-harness.

47
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:58,067
Now the model itself, running locally on a laptop.

48
00:02:58,067 --> 00:03:00,160
We give it three prompts and let it continue.

49
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:03,203
The text is grammatical and keeps the register of the prompt.

50
00:03:03,203 --> 00:03:06,240
It also gets facts wrong and repeats itself, which is

51
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:08,704
what we expect at fifty million parameters.

52
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:18,765
Everything needed to reproduce this is in the repository. uv installs the pinned

53
00:03:18,765 --> 00:03:22,568
environment, and the Modal scripts build the data, train and evaluate.

54
